<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Garifuna</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Garinagu, Black Caribs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Garifuna are a people of mixed African and Indigenous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> descent who formed on Saint Vincent from the 17th century, known to the British as Black Caribs. After their defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-carib-war">Second Carib War</a> most were deported in 1797 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roatan">Roatán</a>, and they spread along the Caribbean coast of Honduras, Belize, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> and Nicaragua. Their language, music and dance are recognised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> as Intangible Cultural Heritage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/garifuna.jpg" alt="Garifuna" width="320" height="345" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Agostino Brunias (ca. 1730 – 2 April 1796) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_Caribs-family-St.-Vincent_by_Augustino_Brunias.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
